The dust clung to Elias like a second skin. It settled in the creases of his weathered hands, coated his thick, grey-flecked beard, and settled in the lines etched around his kind, blue eyes. Elias was a bricklayer, and a damn good one at that. He’d been laying bricks since he was a boy, learning the trade from his father, who’d learned it from his father before him. It was in his blood, in the calloused strength of his hands, in the steady rhythm of his movements.

He wasn't a man of many words. Years spent focused on the precise angle of a brick, the perfect bond of mortar, had honed his focus and quieted his tongue. He preferred the language of brick and mortar, the silent conversation between the materials and his hands. 

Today, he was working on the new community center in the heart of Oakhaven. It was a grand project, a two-story building with arched windows and a sweeping facade. Elias was responsible for the main entrance, a sweeping archway that would welcome everyone into the building. He took immense pride in his work. Each brick was carefully selected, each mortar joint meticulously smoothed. He wasn't just building a wall;
